task: optics-fuse
agent: opus-optics
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/optics-fuse
worktree: ../okay-wt-optics-fuse
spec: specs/optics.md, following optics-fast

why: optics-fast measured WHY a compiled optic is slower and the
  operator read the answer correctly: the indirection is the problem,
  so remove it at compile time instead of at run time. The material is
  already there — `Lens[S](_.f)` expands to `Lens(get, set)` with both
  halves as lambdas, and Scala 3 can beta-reduce them.

what: `Fuse.set` / `Fuse.modify` — macros that read the optic
  EXPRESSION (a chain of `Lens(get, set)`, `Prism.some`, `Prism(...)`
  joined by `andThen`) and emit the nested update straight-line, with
  every lambda beta-reduced and no optic object surviving. A shape the
  macro does not recognise FALLS BACK to the ordinary optic, so it is
  always correct and never a compile error. Laws: fused equals
  unfused on generated values, every family and every shape. JMH: the
  composed `set` against the hand-written nested copy, with -prof gc,
  since bytes are what settled optics-fast.

predict: a fused composed set reaches the hand-written copy (4.5 ns,
  72 B/op) or close; if it does not, the number goes in the spec and
  the macro does not ship. This generates code, which the macro policy
  in specs/codecs.md does not currently allow — if it ships, that
  policy gets its second amendment, stated.
